# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Compiled binaries at the module root. `go build ./cmd/mcp` writes an
# executable named after the package into the working directory — 80 MB of it —
# and one reached the remote on 2026-08-03 before this line existed. Named
# rather than globbed: a bare `mcp` or `api` is unambiguous here, and a glob
# would swallow a future source file that happened to share a binary's name.
/api
/worker
/discord
/mcp

# Output of the go coverage tool
*.out

# Dependency directories
vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work

# Environment variables
.env
.env.local
.env.production
.env.obsolete
# .env.example is intentionally TRACKED — it is the template a new machine or
# contributor needs. It was ignored until the monorepo migration, which is why
# environment setup had become tribal knowledge.

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Build artifacts
/api
/worker
/encrypt_secret
*.o
*.a

# Docker
.dockerignore

# Logs
*.log

# Database
postgres_data/
rabbitmq_data/
redis_data/
metabase_data/

# ngrok
ngrok.yml
!ngrok.yml.example

# NOTE: `docs/` used to be ignored here ("Development Docs"). That meant the API
# reference, the integration guides, and the Postman collection — ~1,100 lines —
# lived only on one machine and were absent from the remote. They are tracked as
# of the monorepo migration. Workspace-level docs live in /docs at the repo root;
# this directory is for backend API reference material.
